WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.028
Hi, and welcome.

00:00:01.028 --> 00:00:05.010
The robotics club is making a poster about a new study space.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:13.617
An AI draft includes a precise percentage and a confident claim about every student, so the team opens the source before printing it.

00:00:13.617 --> 00:00:22.803
As you watch, keep one question in mind: Check the exact claim, the evidence behind it, and the limits of who or what that evidence describes.

00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:25.693
You do not need to memorize a list of terms.

00:00:25.693 --> 00:00:33.144
Try to notice what the people in the story know, what they still need to check, and what choice they can make next.

00:00:33.694 --> 00:00:37.955
Treat each sentence like a small claim that has to pass inspection.

00:00:37.955 --> 00:00:44.824
A source can be real and still fail to support the number, group, date, or cause described in the sentence.

00:00:44.824 --> 00:00:48.386
This comparison is a way to think, not a perfect match.

00:00:48.386 --> 00:00:55.318
Real AI systems can work in different ways, so it helps to ask what this particular tool was designed to do.

00:00:55.318 --> 00:00:58.116
Explain why fluent text can still be wrong.

00:00:58.116 --> 00:01:01.678
What detail in the story makes that goal easier to see?

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:06.414
A second source mentions the same topic but reports a different year.

00:01:06.414 --> 00:01:08.051
Both links open correctly.

00:01:08.051 --> 00:01:12.722
The team still has to check which source matches the claim it plans to make.

00:01:12.722 --> 00:01:13.935
Pause for a moment.

00:01:13.935 --> 00:01:17.393
What changed between the first situation and the second?

00:01:17.393 --> 00:01:21.215
If the result changed too, that is a clue worth investigating.

00:01:21.215 --> 00:01:23.277
It is not proof of the cause yet.

00:01:23.277 --> 00:01:28.676
Think of one more example that could help separate a genuine pattern from a coincidence.

00:01:29.226 --> 00:01:33.644
The club finds a survey with twelve replies, all from robotics members.

00:01:33.644 --> 00:01:39.742
The result may describe those replies, but it cannot automatically stand in for the whole school.

00:01:39.742 --> 00:01:43.103
A useful explanation connects a detail to a decision.

00:01:43.103 --> 00:01:49.014
Try finishing this sentence in your own words: I would pay attention to this detail because...

00:01:49.014 --> 00:01:52.561
Distinguish a relevant source from a merely related one.

00:01:52.561 --> 00:01:56.295
You can use a different example from the one in the lesson.

00:01:56.295 --> 00:02:00.402
The goal is to explain the reasoning, not to repeat a definition.

00:02:00.952 --> 00:02:07.165
Underline the exact claim, find the matching detail in the source, and note who or what was measured.

00:02:07.165 --> 00:02:12.210
If the source does not answer that question, narrow the sentence or leave it out.

00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:16.455
Before looking at any result, say what you expect to happen and why.

00:02:16.455 --> 00:02:19.961
Then compare your prediction with what actually happens.

00:02:19.961 --> 00:02:25.744
If they do not match, keep the surprising result; it may be the most useful part of the test.

00:02:26.294 --> 00:02:30.374
Now imagine the source is an AI-generated summary of another page.

00:02:30.374 --> 00:02:34.948
What original evidence would you want to open before quoting the summary?

00:02:34.948 --> 00:02:38.842
It is tempting to judge a tool by its most impressive example.

00:02:38.842 --> 00:02:45.642
A more careful check also asks who was not represented, what information is missing, and what could go wrong.

00:02:45.642 --> 00:02:49.166
Use a repeatable claim, evidence, and revision workflow.

00:02:49.166 --> 00:02:53.678
A person should be able to question a result and choose what to do next.

00:02:54.228 --> 00:02:57.768
A fictional poll gets seven replies in favor out of ten.

00:02:57.768 --> 00:03:04.532
Say exactly that, name who replied, and avoid claiming that the poll proves a change caused higher grades.

00:03:04.532 --> 00:03:06.555
Work through it in three moves.

00:03:06.555 --> 00:03:09.399
First, say what job the tool is meant to do.

00:03:09.399 --> 00:03:14.773
Next, point to the evidence you have, rather than a guess about how the tool thinks.

00:03:14.773 --> 00:03:17.807
Finally, decide what still needs a human check.

00:03:17.807 --> 00:03:22.548
There may be more than one reasonable answer if you explain your evidence.

00:03:23.098 --> 00:03:27.497
Now open the practice activity, The evidence desk, when you are ready.

00:03:27.497 --> 00:03:33.529
In the evidence activity, trace each statement back to something a reader can actually inspect.

00:03:33.529 --> 00:03:36.922
Keep the wording close to what the evidence measures.

00:03:36.922 --> 00:03:39.498
Take a first try before asking for help.

00:03:39.498 --> 00:03:43.520
If feedback changes your mind, name the detail that changed it.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:47.290
If you keep your answer, explain what evidence supports it.

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.794
Your lesson project is called Write an evidence log.

00:03:50.794 --> 00:03:55.850
For your project, explain how you would check one important statement before sharing it.

00:03:55.850 --> 00:03:59.770
Keep a note of what the source supports and what is still uncertain.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:04.087
Before you finish, imagine someone else using your idea for the first time.

00:04:04.087 --> 00:04:05.792
What would they need to know?

00:04:05.792 --> 00:04:08.859
What could they do if the tool did not work for them?

00:04:08.859 --> 00:04:11.984
Those questions can make a promising idea more useful.

00:04:12.534 --> 00:04:17.617
Pick one word from today's lesson and explain it using the story you just heard.

00:04:17.617 --> 00:04:20.349
Avoid copying the wording from the screen.

00:04:20.349 --> 00:04:23.844
You might begin, In this situation, this word means...

00:04:23.844 --> 00:04:27.656
If your explanation is hard to follow, add a small example.

00:04:27.656 --> 00:04:33.438
Clear language is a good way to notice what you understand and what you want to ask about.

00:04:33.988 --> 00:04:41.862
Here is one idea to take with you: Check the exact claim, the evidence behind it, and the limits of who or what that evidence describes.

00:04:41.862 --> 00:04:46.667
Think of a different class or school situation where the same question could help.

00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:51.009
You can write down one thing you would check and one person you could ask.

00:04:51.009 --> 00:04:55.756
That is enough for a useful next step; you do not have to solve every part today.

00:04:56.306 --> 00:04:58.773
Thanks for thinking this through with me.

00:04:58.773 --> 00:05:04.789
Finish the activity and project on the lesson page, then try the separate quiz when you feel ready.

00:05:04.789 --> 00:05:10.745
If a question feels difficult, return to the example and explain your reasoning in your own words.

00:05:10.745 --> 00:05:16.881
Check the exact claim, the evidence behind it, and the limits of who or what that evidence describes.

00:05:16.881 --> 00:05:19.228
The goal is not to trust every answer.

00:05:19.228 --> 00:05:23.138
It is to know what to check and how to make a thoughtful choice.
